{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000327",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS000327, PGS002790). PGS000327 has the strongest endpoint fidelity: its phenotyping_reported is explicitly “Autism spectrum disorder,” matching trait_reported/trait_efo, and it comes from an ASD-focused publication (“Identification of common genetic risk variants for autism spectrum disorder.”) with multi-cohort development and larger validation (n=7,148). It also shows higher reported variance explained (full_model_r2=0.0245) and a per-SD effect size (OR=1.33, 95% CI 1.30–1.36). In contrast, PGS002790 has endpoint incoherence for the target because phenotyping_reported is “Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn)” despite the ASD trait label, and its reported partial R² is smaller (0.00546) with smaller validation (n=2,198). The overall outcome is DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL rather than high quality because neither model provides clearly PRS-only AUROC/R² (metrics are reported as full_model_r2 / partial R²), limiting cross-model comparability and certainty about standalone PRS performance."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000452",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 20 candidates are direct matches on trait_reported/trait_efo (basal cell carcinoma). Most provide only full-model AUROC (with basic covariates), so PRS-only comparability is limited and lowers certainty. Within the coherent Cancer PRSweb family (many closely related BCC models with similar EUR evaluation and age/sex/PC covariates), PGS000452 has the strongest visible performance package: full-model AUROC=0.632 (95% CI 0.616–0.647), the highest reported Nagelkerke R²=0.0487, and a strong top-1% vs rest OR=3.61, all in European validation (n=11,322). Competing models include PGS000454/456/458 with nearly identical AUROC/R² and similar covariates, making the top choice not uniquely dominant; meanwhile PGS000119 reports higher AUROC (0.64) but lacks R² and comes from a different PRSWeb study context. Newer Nat Commun 2023/2025 scores (PGS003416/PGS003417/PGS005207) lack comparable AUROC/R², and PGS005207 is endpoint-restricted to childhood cancer survivors, so they are less suitable for a generic BCC target in this direct-match stage."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002237",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 22 candidates are direct EFO matches to chronic kidney disease, but they vary materially in endpoint fidelity and comparability of reported performance. PGS002237 is preferred because it is from a disease-focused PRS study explicitly targeting CKD prediction across ancestries (Nat Med), uses a clear CKD stage ≥3-style endpoint via eGFR <45 (high endpoint fidelity for clinically meaningful CKD), has large training (n=279,819) and multi-ancestry evaluation, and reports supportive effect size (OR=1.47) alongside a reported AUROC=0.78 in European validation. However, the evidence remains sub-optimal because the AUROC shown appears to be a full-model metric with many clinical/mediator covariates (hypertension, diabetes, BMI, medication use, etc.), and many other candidates lack PRS-only metrics, making cross-model comparability limited; additionally, several later multi-method UKB comparative models (e.g., PGS004158) report cleaner covariate handling (covariates=0) but only modest AUROC (~0.59) and represent a different endpoint framing (“CKD or dialysis”), so no single model is unambiguously best on fully comparable PRS-only performance."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000639",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for Hodgkin’s lymphoma are present (PGS000637/PGS000638/PGS000639/PGS000640 with phenotyping_reported=\"Hodgkin's disease\" and trait_efo=\"Hodgkins lymphoma\"). Among these, PGS000639 (P+T; 20 variants) has the strongest reported validation performance within the same publication/family and evaluation setting (full_model_auc=0.62; Nagelkerke R²=0.0276; OR per SD=1.476 [1.154–1.889]) compared with the other Cancer PRSweb Hodgkin’s disease scores (AUC 0.569–0.601; R² 0.0109–0.0193; OR 1.292–1.377). However, overall evidence remains limited: validation sample size is small (n=775), only full-model AUROC is reported (no explicit PRS-only AUROC), and an additional Hodgkin lymphoma-labeled model (PGS003449) appears endpoint-incoherent in metadata (phenotyping_reported=\"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia\") with no usable performance metrics, reducing confidence in the broader catalog signal for this trait."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000639",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match Hodgkin’s lymphoma candidates are present (PGS000637–PGS000640; plus PGS003449 but with endpoint-incoherent metadata). Among the coherent Hodgkin’s disease PRSweb set (same publication, same EUR ancestry, same validation n=775, similar covariates), PGS000639 (P+T, 20 variants) has the strongest reported discrimination and association: full-model AUROC 0.62 (95% CI 0.559–0.688), highest Nagelkerke R² (0.0276), and the largest per-score OR 1.476 (95% CI 1.154–1.889) versus the other three. However, confidence is limited because the validation sample is small (n=775), metrics appear to be full-model (not explicit PRS-only), and the newer Hodgkin lymphoma-labeled PGS003449 has conflicting phenotype fields (phenotyping_reported = chronic lymphocytic leukemia), making it unusable despite the exact label."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000078",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates exist for lung cancer (n=35). PGS000078 has strong phenotype alignment (trait_reported: Lung cancer; trait_efo: lung carcinoma; phenotyping_reported: Incident lung cancer) and is supported by a disease-relevant, large external evaluation context (EVAL EUR 100% with validation_sample_size n=392,539) with reported discrimination (full_model AUROC=0.846; C-index=0.849; HR=1.16). Competing models either have weaker endpoint fidelity (e.g., early-onset only: PGS000740; never-smokers only: PGS004164/PGS004165), much weaker reported discrimination in the provided metrics (e.g., Cancer PRSweb lung/bronchus models with AUROC ~0.52–0.54), or lack comparable performance metrics despite large variant counts (several LDpred2/PRS-CS models with missing AUROC/R2). However, this selection remains sub-optimal because the available performance metric is a full-model AUROC with extensive covariate adjustment (including smoking intensity/duration, PM2.5, family history and interaction terms), and no explicit PRS-only AUROC/R2 is provided here; thus the high AUROC may be substantially covariate-driven and is not directly comparable across candidates."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003580",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 24 candidates are concept-level direct matches to major depressive disorder by trait_efo, but several have endpoint or evaluation mismatches that weaken them for generic MDD PRS selection (e.g., PGS000767 validates against putamen volume; PGS000907 validates against an antidepressant side-effect phenotype; PGS002789/PGS003442 validate against cognition/behavior/neuroimaging readouts rather than MDD diagnosis). Among endpoint-faithful options, the 2023 bioRxiv cluster (PGS003576–PGS003585) consistently targets lifetime MDD with the same validation setting (European; n=42,250 for most), providing a coherent, disease-focused family. Within that family, PGS003580 has one of the highest reported R² values (0.0254) with standard PC covariates, edging very similar near-clones (e.g., PGS003578 R²=0.0257; PGS003576 R²=0.0241), but the small performance differences and lack of clearly PRS-only metrics/AUROC for this family prevent a clearly definitive pick. Other candidates either have lower reported R²/AUROC (e.g., PGS000138 full-model AUROC=0.561; R²=0.018) or report non-comparable metrics/endpoints (e.g., PGS004591 reports HR in an air-pollution context; PGS004885 is an incident/time-to-event framing with different metric type). Hence a direct match exists, but evidence remains insufficiently decisive for a high-confidence single best choice."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003578","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for major depressive disorder are present (24 total). Several have endpoint-aligned phenotyping_reported as MDD status/lifetime MDD/incident MDD (e.g., PGS000138/139/145, PGS003576–PGS003585, PGS003333, PGS004885, PGS002759). Multiple candidates are not endpoint-faithful despite matching trait_efo (e.g., PGS000767 uses putamen volume; PGS000907 is evaluated on fluoxetine-taker muscle pain; PGS002789 and PGS003442 use cognition/behavior/neuroimaging phenotypes), so they are down-ranked for phenotype mismatch.

Among endpoint-faithful models, the strongest coherent cluster is the 2023 ‘Phenotype integration improves power and preserves specificity in biobank-based genetic studies of MDD’ family (PGS003576–PGS003585): they share the same MDD endpoint, consistent basic covariates (PCs 1–10), large validation (n=42,250 for most), and provide comparable R² metrics. Within this cluster, PGS003578 has the highest reported R² (0.0257) with the larger validation set (n=42,250), making it the best-supported choice from the visible evidence.

Main limitation: performance reporting is heterogeneous and often not clearly PRS-only (mostly generic R²; many AUCs missing), and an outlier metric (e.g., PGS000907 reporting 0.77 ‘Nagelkerke R²*100’ on a non-MDD endpoint) highlights comparability concerns; thus confidence is not High despite a clear preferred family/variant."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002037",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS001830, PGS002037), both aligned to the target via trait_efo=\"nicotine dependence\" and phenotyping_reported/trait_reported=\"Tobacco use disorder\", from the same portability framework publication and with the same training cohort (UKB; n=391,124), similar covariates, and identical reported validation sample size (n=19,400). The main differentiator in the visible evidence is PRS-comparable performance: PGS002037 has higher partial correlation (partial-r=0.0741, CI 0.0601–0.0881) than PGS001830 (partial-r=0.0557, CI 0.0416–0.0697) in the selected European validation record, so it is the best-supported choice among the two. However, confidence is limited because both models come from a pan-trait portability framework and lack standard comparable metrics (AUC/R2 are null), leaving only partial-r for comparison and no external, disease-focused validation evidence in the provided context."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002046",
  "confidence": "Low",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (both map to trait_efo=otosclerosis). PGS002046 is preferred because it uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (LDpred2) with substantially larger training size (n=391,124 vs n=269,704) and a direct otosclerosis phenotype, whereas PGS001255 is a sparse snpnet score from a pan-trait UK Biobank framework (\"across 813 traits\"), which domain guidance flags as having a lower prior for per-disease deployment despite having more complete AUROC reporting. However, the evidence is insufficient for a high-quality selection because both are framework-style UKB-derived (not disease-focused multi-cohort), and PGS002046 lacks standard PRS-comparable AUC/R² metrics (only partial-r=0.0182), making performance comparability and endpoint/metric interpretation uncertain."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001255",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (both trait_efo=otosclerosis). PGS001255 has clearer, more directly comparable discrimination reporting for European validation (PGS-only AUROC=0.6319 with CI; full-model AUROC=0.6532; incremental AUROC=0.05946; PGS R2(no covariates)=0.01341), plus larger validation sample size (n=67,425) than PGS002046 (n=19,770), whose performance is only reported as partial correlation (partial-r=0.0182) with no AUROC/R2. However, both models come from UK Biobank pan-trait/portability framework papers (snpnet across 813 traits; portability of 245 scores), and PGS001255’s endpoint is time-to-event (phenotyping_reported: TTE otosclerosis), which the domain policy suggests down-ranking versus generic disease endpoints absent stronger disease-focused evidence. Given this framework context and non-identical endpoint definitions, the selection is supported but not high-certainty."
}

