{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005217",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct/near-direct matches for the target concept (peripheral vascular disease), with two labeled as peripheral vascular disease (PGS001843, PGS002055) and two as peripheral artery disease (PGS005158, PGS005217), which is a closely aligned large-vessel subtype. PGS005217 is best supported overall because it has a disease-focused publication context (\"Polygenic Prediction of Peripheral Artery Disease and Major Adverse Limb Events\"), a very large multi-cohort validation sample (n=304,294) with multi-ancestry evaluation, clean/basic covariates (age, sex, 10 PCs), and strong effect-size evidence (HR 1.66, 95% CI 1.61–1.71). By contrast, the two UKB portability-framework models (PGS001843/PGS002055) report only small partial correlations (0.0151–0.0175) and come from a pan-trait portability paper, and PGS005158 is restricted to a type 2 diabetes PAD phenotype with extensive clinical/lifestyle covariates and a smaller validation (n=10,836). Outcome is DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL (not HIGH_QUALITY) because the selected model’s endpoint is peripheral artery disease rather than the broader/unspecified peripheral vascular disease label, and the only discrimination metric shown is a full-model C-index (0.731) rather than an explicitly PRS-only AUC/R2, limiting direct comparability across candidates."
}

{
