  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present (3 models). PGS000922 and PGS000923 are exact sarcoidosis EFO matches with similar endpoint/covariate context (UKB-only snpnet framework; covariates: age, sex, array, PCs; European validation), and both report PRS-only AUROC/R2 plus full-model AUROC. PGS000922 is preferred only marginally because it matches the generic disease endpoint without time-to-event packaging (PGS000923 is explicitly time-to-event), while reported discrimination is very close (full-model AUROC 0.6486 vs 0.65455). PGS001872 appears less aligned because its trait_efo is 'skin sarcoidosis' (subtype mismatch to generic sarcoidosis) and it lacks AUROC/R2 (only partial-r). Overall evidence is limited because the top two candidates come from a pan-trait UKB framework publication rather than a disease-focused sarcoidosis study, and the choice between the two snpnet models is not clearly resolved by the small performance differences."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005220",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present for sleep apnea, including multiple generic “Sleep apnea” ExPRSweb scores (PGS003204–PGS003218) and several disease-focused “Obstructive sleep apnea” (OSA) scores (PGS003479, PGS003857/PGS003858, PGS005219/PGS005220). Using the provided domain guidance that OSA is the clinically dominant subtype of sleep apnea and can be preferred when better supported, the most supported options are the 2025 EBioMedicine disease-focused multi-cohort OSA scores (PGS005219/PGS005220): they have clear endpoint alignment (“Obstructive sleep apnea”), multi-cohort development (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP), multi-ancestry evaluation noted, and substantially stronger reported discrimination (full_model AUROC 0.79) and effect sizes (OR 1.53–1.98) than the ExPRSweb “Sleep apnea” cluster where full-model AUROCs are ~0.49–0.527. Between the two 2025 scores, PGS005220 is preferred for generic deployment because it is the unadjusted-BMI version, avoiding explicit mediator-adjustment labeling that can reduce general comparability; however, both PGS005219/PGS005220 report only full-model AUROC (no PGS-only/incremental metrics), so the PRS contribution cannot be cleanly isolated and this keeps the overall direct-match recommendation sub-optimal rather than high-quality."
}
